0.00019621849060058594, 0.00588226318359375, 0.0166168212890625, 0.01242828369140625, -0.01092529296875, -0.005023956298828125, -0.036285400390625, -0.00681304931640625, -0.0179901123046875, 0.004222869873046875, 0.0123443603515625, -0.003978729248046875, -0.01239776611328125, 0.0002522468566894531, 0.003681182861328125, -0.007373809814453125, -0.0168304443359375, 0.008209228515625, 0.034942626953125, -0.0256500244140625, -0.02581787109375, -0.006221771240234375, -0.00522613525390625, -0.01922607421875, 0.01488494873046875, 0.0007205009460449219, -0.005016326904296875, 0.01166534423828125, -0.0286102294921875, -0.01611328125, -0.01959228515625, -0.01068878173828125, 0.019439697265625, -0.003017425537109375, 0.0200042724609375, -0.03985595703125, -0.0031757354736328125, -0.0341796875, -0.0133209228515625, -0.008819580078125, 0.01177978515625, -0.015289306640625, -0.01346588134765625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Permanental processes are a class of stochastic point process models that have been used in spatial statistics and machine learning, particularly for modeling repulsion between points (McCullagh & M\u00f8ller, 2006). However, standard permanental process models typically do not accommodate fixed effects, limiting their ability to incorporate known covariate influences or domain knowledge. In this work, we address this limitation by extending the permanental process model to include fixed effects, thereby enabling interpretable modeling of covariate information alongside nonparametric spatial structure.\n\nIntroducing fixed effects into permanental processes raises several statistical and computational challenges. Statistically, the inclusion of both fixed effects and a flexible nonparametric component can lead to identifiability issues, especially if covariates are highly correlated with features captured by the kernel. Overfitting may also occur if the prior on the fixed effects is too diffuse, particularly in small-sample settings. Computationally, the penalized likelihood involves an intractable integral over the domain, and the joint optimization over both \\(\\beta\\) and \\(f\\) increases complexity compared to standard models.\n\nOur main contribution is to formulate a permanental process model for a point process on a domain \\( \\mathcal{X} \\) with an intensity function of the form\n\\[\n\\lambda(x) = \\exp\\left( \\beta^\\top z(x) + f(x) \\right),\n\\]\nwhere \\( z(x) \\) is a vector of known covariates at location \\( x \\), \\( \\beta \\) is a vector of fixed effect coefficients, and \\( f \\) is a function in a Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS) associated with a positive definite kernel \\( k \\). This extends the standard permanental process, which typically models \\( \\lambda(x) \\) as a function in an RKHS without explicit fixed effects. By including \\( \\beta^\\top z(x) \\), our model allows for direct incorporation of interpretable covariate effects, distinguishing it from prior work such as McCullagh & M\u00f8ller (2006), where such fixed effects are not present.\n\nWe place a Gaussian prior on \\( f \\) and a Gaussian prior with variance \\( \\sigma^2 \\) on \\( \\beta \\). The choice of prior variance for \\( \\beta \\) is critical: a very large (diffuse) prior allows the data to determine the fixed effects but can lead to identifiability problems and overfitting, especially when covariates are weakly informative or collinear with the kernel features. In practice, we recommend selecting \\( \\sigma^2 \\) via cross-validation or empirical Bayes methods, balancing flexibility and regularization. Sensitivity analysis with respect to \\( \\sigma^2 \\) can help assess the robustness of inference. In our synthetic experiments (see below), we observe that moderate regularization (\\( \\sigma^2 \\) chosen by cross-validation) improves predictive performance and interpretability compared to the diffuse prior limit.\n\nFor inference, we use penalized likelihood estimation. The log-likelihood for observed points \\( \\{x_i\\}_{i=1}^n \\) is\n\\[\n\\ell(\\beta, f) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left[ \\beta^\\top z(x_i) + f(x_i) \\right] - \\int_{\\mathcal{X}} \\exp\\left( \\beta^\\top z(x) + f(x) \\right) dx.\n\\]\nWe maximize the penalized log-likelihood\n\\[\n\\ell(\\beta, f) - \\frac{1}{2} \\|f\\|^2_{\\mathcal{H}_k} - \\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2} \\|\\beta\\|^2,\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k} \\) is the RKHS norm and \\( \\sigma^2 \\) is the prior variance for \\( \\beta \\). In the diffuse prior limit (\\( \\sigma^2 \\to \\infty \\)), the penalty on \\( \\beta \\) vanishes. The representer theorem (see Wahba, 1990, Theorem 1.1) ensures that the solution for \\( f \\) lies in the span of kernel functions evaluated at the observed data points, provided the loss and penalty are strictly convex and the kernel is positive definite. Thus, the estimated intensity function decomposes into a sum of a fixed effects term and an RKHS function, with the RKHS norm controlling the complexity of the nonparametric component.\n\nIn practice, we optimize the penalized likelihood using alternating minimization: we fix \\( \\beta \\) and solve for \\( f \\) (which reduces to a finite-dimensional problem by the representer theorem), then fix \\( f \\) and update \\( \\beta \\) via gradient-based methods. The integral over \\( \\mathcal{X} \\) is approximated using numerical quadrature or Monte Carlo integration, depending on the domain. For large datasets, sparse kernel approximations or stochastic optimization can be used to improve scalability.\n\nThis decomposition facilitates scientific interpretation: the fixed effects \\( \\beta \\) are directly related to interpretable covariates, while the RKHS component \\( f \\) captures residual structure. For example, in ecological applications, environmental variables can be included as fixed effects, while spatial dependence is modeled via the RKHS component. However, the model assumes that covariates are correctly specified and that the kernel is appropriate for the data. If these assumptions are violated, model performance may suffer. We recommend model diagnostics such as residual analysis, cross-validation for kernel and covariate selection, and sensitivity analysis to assess robustness. Information criteria (e.g., AIC, BIC) can also guide model selection.\n\nTo illustrate the method, we present a synthetic example. We simulate points on \\([0,1]^2\\) with an intensity function depending on a linear covariate and a spatially varying component. Our method accurately recovers the true fixed effect coefficient and captures the residual spatial structure, while omitting the fixed effect leads to biased estimates and poor predictive performance. Details and code are provided in the supplement.\n\nOur approach is related to semiparametric models in the literature (see Wahba, 1990), which combine parametric and nonparametric components, but differs from standard permanental process models by explicitly incorporating fixed effects. Unlike McCullagh & M\u00f8ller (2006), who model intensity solely via an RKHS function, our model separates interpretable covariate effects from residual structure, improving interpretability and flexibility. Compared to other semiparametric point process models, our approach leverages the repulsive properties of permanental processes while enabling covariate adjustment.\n\nIn summary, we extend the permanental process model to include fixed effects with a tunable prior, providing a flexible framework that balances interpretability and nonparametric modeling. This enables straightforward incorporation of domain knowledge and offers a principled way to model both covariate effects and residual structure. Future work includes large-scale empirical evaluation, theoretical analysis of identifiability and regularization, and methodological extensions such as hierarchical modeling and automated kernel selection."}}